// Fit Feet's Course Details hero (675:8156 desktop / 999:7170 mobile) has a
// real 4-pill "Language" selector (English/Spanish/French/Japanese) this
// file had no field or render branch for at all — the 9th finding on this
// branch, reported not fixed by the task that first authored that item's
// data. Rather than adding a second, parallel `languages` field next to the
// existing single-pill tier field (which would just re-hardcode "one label
// + N pills" a level up), the pilot's tier block — already exactly that
// shape with N pinned at 1 — is generalized in place:
//
//   OLD single-purpose field: { label: href pill }
//   -> pdp.pills: { label, options: [{ label, href?, selected? }] }
//
// `label` is real per-course Figma data now (read off each course's OWN
// node, never assumed) — Sole Switch/Sole Switch Pro's frames both read
// "Select your course" (migrated verbatim, just reshaped); Fit Feet's reads
// "Language". `options` is 1 element for the migrated courses (unchanged
// content, just moved), 4 for Fit Feet. Combating Bunions sets no `pills`
// at all (its own frames show neither), so the whole block still renders
// nothing there — the exact guard-the-whole-wrapper fix #1 above already
// established, now keyed off `pills`.
//
// Interactive vs inert, per option: an option with an `href` renders as a
// real `<a>` (Sole Switch/Sole Switch Pro's single cross-link pill, real
// navigation, unchanged behavior). An option WITHOUT `href` renders as a
// plain, non-interactive `<span>` — verified in Figma that neither language
// pill links anywhere in either frame (each is an unlinked "Button" node,
// no prototype interaction): this is a Kajabi/Shopify-side language-variant
// control this reference build doesn't wire up. A `<button>` styled inert
// would still promise "this is a control" to assistive tech; a control that
// does nothing is a false affordance — the exact reasoning
// Testimonial.astro's decorative (`aria-hidden`, non-`<button>`) arrows
// already established for a single-testimonial page, applied here the same
// way (though unlike those purely-iconographic arrows, the language names
// ARE meaningful text content, so these spans are NOT `aria-hidden` — only
// their non-interactivity is inherited from that precedent, not their
// invisibility to assistive tech). `English`'s filled/selected state is
// real Figma data (`selected: true`), reflected as a `--selected` modifier
// class (fills the pill, reusing the same 1px teal border so its box stays
// the same size as its outline siblings — no extra CSS needed to prevent a
// size jump). See the "Review fix wave" section below for how the
// selected state is exposed to assistive tech (superseding an earlier
// `aria-current`-on-`<span>` approach) and for the markup's actual
// two-level shape.
//
// ---- Review fix wave (pill-group findings) -------------------------------
// Two follow-up fixes to the pill-group generalization above, caught on
// review before this branch merged:
//
// 1. Markup shape / geometry. The first pass deliberately flattened
//    Figma's two-level structure (675:8156;181:819 column > nested
//    181:822 pill row) into a single `.course-details__tier` flex-wrap
//    container, reasoning that the tradeoff (a wrong 12px gap between
//    WRAPPED pill rows, vs Figma's 8px) was worth staying byte-identical
//    with the two already-shipped Sole Switch pages. That traded away
//    correctness on the one page this generalization exists to build
//    (`/courses/fit-feet`, the only course whose pills actually wrap) to
//    preserve byte-identity on two pages that don't need it — the wrong
//    side of that tradeoff. Fixed by adding the `.course-details__pills`
//    wrapper Figma actually has: `.course-details__tier` is now a column
//    (`gap: var(--space-3)`, i.e. 12px, label-to-pills) containing
//    `.course-details__pills`, a flex-wrap row (`gap: var(--space-2)`,
//    8px) around just the options — so 8px now governs wrapped-row gaps
//    too, matching Figma exactly. The label's `flex: 1 0 100%` line-break
//    hack is gone (the column layout makes it redundant). Sole Switch/Sole
//    Switch Pro now differ from their previously-shipped HTML by exactly
//    one wrapper `<div>` — same label text, same pill(s), same href,
//    same computed geometry (their pill count never wraps, so the 12-vs-8
//    gap was never visible there either way).
//
// 2. `aria-current="true"` on the inert `<span>` (the language pills) was
//    the wrong instrument: a role-less generic `<span>` doesn't reliably
//    surface "current" state to screen readers (no mappable role, and the
//    four pills aren't exposed as a set), leaving `English`'s selected
//    state carried by background fill alone — a WCAG 1.4.1 colour-only
//    smell. Replaced with a visually-hidden " (selected)" text node inside
//    the selected span, `.course-details__sr-only` — byte-for-byte the
//    same clip-rect utility `.comparison-chart__sr-only` /
//    `.star-rating__sr-only` / `.testimonial__sr-only` already establish
//    in this repo (now a 4th instance of the same idiom, not a new one).
//    `aria-current="true"` moved to the real `<a>` branch instead (Sole
//    Switch/Sole Switch Pro's cross-link pill) — the canonically correct,
//    universally-supported element for it; a no-op today since no shipped
//    `<a>` option also sets `selected: true`, but no longer backwards.
//
// 3. MINOR — the `pills` guard checked presence (`item.pdp?.pills &&`),
//    not emptiness. `pills: { label, options: [] }` would have re-created
//    the exact "caption with nothing under it" bug the courseCard/heroImage
//    guard fix (above) already killed once, and `pills: { label }` with no
//    `options` key would throw at build (`.map` on `undefined`). Guard is
//    now `item.pdp?.pills?.options?.length > 0`, which also subsumes the
//    old presence check.
//
// ---- Hero caption (Chunk B2 follow-up: component gap fix) ---------------
// Gait Foundations' Course Details hero (675:9037 desktop / 1116:14701
// mobile) shows a short credibility-stat line, "*900+ Professionals
// Certified", in its OWN <p> directly above the intro paragraph — confirmed
// via get_design_context on both breakpoints that each frame's own text
// node (675:9037;173:130 desktop / 1116:14701;181:664 mobile) is a single
// wrapper containing exactly these two <p> children, not one paragraph with
// the stat folded into its text. This file had no field or render path for
// a second caption line at all (`description` renders exactly one
// paragraph), so the line was reported (not fixed) by the task that first
// authored gait-foundations' own catalog data — fixed here per that same
// "report now, generalize later" precedent this file's `pills`/`courseCard`
// /`heroImage` fields above already followed.
//
// `item.pdp.heroCaption` is a new OPTIONAL plain string, read/guarded
// exactly like `description`/`instructorsByline` already are on this same
// file — a bare `&&` check. An empty string is already falsy in JS, so no
// extra `.length`/emptiness check is needed the way `pills.options` and
// `threeColumn.columns` (both arrays, where `{ options: [] }` would slip
// past a bare presence check) require one. Absent (every course before
// Gait Foundations) renders NOTHING — not even a wrapper element — so this
// is a byte-identical no-op for every other shipped PDP.
//
// Markup: when present, `heroCaption` and `description` share a nested
// `.course-details__intro-block` wrapper, mirroring Figma's own single
// text-block node that holds both paragraphs, so their own tight 8px gap
// (`--space-2`, Figma's literal `mb-[8px]` on the caption `<p>`) is
// independent of the buybox's outer --space-5 (24px) rhythm governing the
// gap to this block's neighbors — the same "nested wrapper carries its own
// tighter internal gap" pattern `.course-details__tier`/
// `.course-details__pills` already established for the pill-group above.
// When `heroCaption` is unset, `description` renders unwrapped exactly as
// before (the same lone `<p>`, no extra `<div>`) — required for the ten
// already-shipped PDPs to stay byte-identical.
//
// Typography: Figma's own text-block node uses ONE font/colour for both
// paragraphs (type/body-sm, `--text-primary`) — the caption isn't bold, a
// different colour, or a caption/eyebrow style; it's a second body-sm
// paragraph, just tightly spaced above the first. `.course-details__caption`
// therefore reuses `.course-details__intro`'s exact font/colour
// declarations rather than inventing a new type style.
//
// ---- Select control (Chunk B2 review fix wave: component gap fix) -------
// (NOTE: `pills`/`pills.control` are likewise superseded by `buybox` — see
// the "Buy box controls generalization" section at the end of this header
// comment. The history below is preserved as-is, unedited, for the record.)
// Trainer Certification's own buybox (683:10088 desktop / 1116:17399 mobile,
// both node `...;181:1352`/`;181:1388`, "Course Type") shows "SELECT YOUR
// COURSE" over a real bordered dropdown — placeholder "Location…" + a
// chevron — not a pill/button. The task that first authored this course's
// catalog data correctly found `pills` had no render path for this (only
// button-styled options, real `<a>` links or inert `<span>`s) and left the
// field unset rather than force-fitting a select into a pill. This is fixed
// here by EXTENDING `pills` rather than adding a parallel field — the same
// generalization move as the pill-group section above (`pills` itself was
// once a single-purpose tier field, generalized in place instead of
// growing a second, parallel one) — via a new `control: 'pills' | 'select'`
// key, defaulting to `'pills'` so all ten already-shipped courses (which
// never set `control` at all) take the exact same branch and render the
// exact same markup as before this fix.
//
// Guard: NOT the same emptiness check for both control types. `'pills'`
// keeps the pre-existing `options.length > 0` guard verbatim. `'select'` is
// guarded on `label` alone — Trainer Certification's own `options` array is
// DELIBERATELY empty (Figma exposes only the closed/placeholder state of
// this control, with no option list behind it to pull), so gating on
// `options.length` here would hide a real, Figma-verbatim control behind the
// same guard that (correctly) hides an accidentally-empty pill list. This is
// a documented stub for the porting team, not a false affordance: the
// select renders with its own real Figma-verbatim placeholder text, an
// empty option list beneath it (see catalog.js's own comment on this field
// — the client must supply the real location list; none is invented here).
//
// Markup: a real native `<select>`, not a fake bordered `<div>` — see
// `.course-details__select`'s own CSS comment for why it reuses
// FilterSortBar.astro's exact select styling/precedent (including that
// file's choice not to add a custom chevron overlay) rather than a new
// styling approach.
//
// ---- Buy box controls generalization (review fix wave: pills -> buybox) --
// Functional Gait Assessment Level 1's own "Course Type" block (675:9358
// node `I675:9358;181:1352` desktop / 1116:15572 node `I1116:15572;
// 181:1388` mobile) shows BOTH a 2-option pill row ("Online On-Demand"
// outline / "In-Person" filled-selected) AND a "Location…" select beneath
// it, under ONE shared label — a combination the `pills` field (as of the
// "Select control" section above) could not express: `pillsControl` picked
// EXACTLY ONE branch, pills OR select, never both. This is the 15th finding
// on this branch (Task 5 correctly found it and left `pills` unset rather
// than guess which control to drop).
//
// This field has now been generalized THREE times — `secondaryCta` (a
// single link) -> `pills` (a label + N button-styled options) -> `pills`
// + `control: 'select'` (a label + EITHER an option list OR a select) —
// and every widening changed the control's TYPE, never its CARDINALITY.
// That was the actual missing axis: a course hero can need MORE THAN ONE
// control under one shared label. Rather than keep the block-level
// `control` flag and bolt on a second, parallel block (which would either
// duplicate the shared label or force an awkward `pills2` field), `pills`
// is renamed to `buybox` (the name was a lie the moment it grew
// `control: 'select'` — a select isn't a pill) and reshaped to a label
// governing an ORDERED LIST of `controls`:
//
//   OLD: { label, options }                     -> pills, ONE control
//   OLD: { label, control: 'select', ... }       -> select, ONE control
//   NEW: { label, controls: [ { type, ... } ] }  -> N controls, any mix
//
// `controls` is NOT an array of `{ label, options }` GROUPS — that would
// duplicate the one shared label Figma itself shows exactly once per block.
// Each entry is just the TYPE-SPECIFIC payload (`type: 'pills'` + `options`,
// or `type: 'select'` + `placeholder`/`options`), rendered in array order —
// Figma's own top-to-bottom order (pill row, then select) is preserved by
// authoring the array in that same order, not by any special-casing in this
// component.
//
// MIGRATION: every existing `pills` user is migrated MECHANICALLY, with
// byte-identical rendered output (see each item's own comment in
// catalog.js for its specific before/after):
//   { label, options }                                  ->
//     { label, controls: [{ type: 'pills', options }] }
//   { label, control: 'select', placeholder, options }  ->
//     { label, controls: [{ type: 'select', placeholder, options }] }
//
// GUARDS ARE NOW PER-CONTROL, NOT PER-BLOCK — this is where the old code
// was already fragile (the select's guard keyed on the SHARED `label`,
// which was fine when a block only ever had one control, but would have
// been wrong the moment two controls shared that label: an empty select
// sitting next to a real pill group would have rendered anyway, keyed off
// the pill group's own label instead of its own emptiness). Each control
// now survives or dies on its OWN non-emptiness check — `pills`:
// `options.length > 0`; `select`: `placeholder || options.length > 0`
// (Trainer Certification's/this course's own select both have
// `options: []` by design, so `placeholder` alone still lets the
// documented-stub control render). The whole `.course-details__tier` block
// renders iff AT LEAST ONE control survives its own check; the shared
// `<span>` label itself is separately guarded on its own non-emptiness (an
// empty/absent `buybox.label` renders no label, even if a control still
// renders) — no empty wrapper, label, `<select>`, or pill row ever leaks
// through either guard.
//
// A11Y: the select used to take `aria-label={pills.label}` — safe when a
// select was the ONLY control under that label, but under a label SHARED
// with a pill group (this course's own case) that would give the select
// the exact same accessible name as the adjacent pill group ("Course Type"
// naming both), the exact ambiguity `aria-label` exists to prevent.
// `selectAccessibleName()` below defaults to the shared block `label` —
// BYTE-IDENTICAL to Trainer Certification's already-shipped select, which
// authors no override — but a control-level `ariaLabel` can opt out of that
// default. This course's own select is the one control that needs to: it
// authors `ariaLabel: 'Location'` (see catalog.js), distinct from its
// block's shared "Course Type" label, so the two adjacent controls under
// one caption are never given the same accessible name.
//
// Markup/CSS: UNCHANGED. `.course-details__tier`'s existing column layout
// (`flex-direction: column`, `gap: var(--space-3)`) already gives a uniform
// 12px gap between the label and EVERY child after it — Figma's own
// "Course Type" node (I675:9358;181:1352) is itself a single gap-12 column
// containing the label, then the pill row, then the select, so rendering
// multiple controls as additional siblings in that same column reproduces
// Figma exactly with no new CSS needed. `.course-details__pills`/
// `.course-details__select`/`.course-details__pill*` are reused verbatim,
// unchanged, per control.
import StarRating from '../plp/StarRating.astro';
import { isExternalHref } from '../../scripts/links.js';

const { item } = Astro.props;
// Enroll CTA — externality derived from the href itself, reusing the shared
// `isExternalHref` predicate (`src/scripts/links.js`, also used by
// ComparisonChart.astro's own CTA — see that file's "Internal-vs-external
// CTA fix" header comment). All three shipped courses' `enrollHref` values
// are external Kajabi URLs, so this stays a no-op today; a future
// internal-route `enrollHref` would now correctly omit `target`/`rel`
// instead of the previous hardcoded pair.
const enrollHref = item.pdp?.enrollHref ?? '#';
const isExternal = isExternalHref(enrollHref);

// Buy box — see this file's own "Buy box controls generalization" header
// comment for the full history/reasoning. `buybox.controls` is normalized
// (default `type: 'pills'`, per-control non-emptiness `show` flag computed)
// then filtered down to only the controls that survive their OWN guard —
// NOT one guard for the whole block.
const buybox = item.pdp?.buybox;
const buyboxControls = (buybox?.controls ?? [])
  .map((control) => {
    const type = control.type === 'select' ? 'select' : 'pills';
    const show =
      type === 'select'
        ? Boolean(control.placeholder) || (control.options?.length ?? 0) > 0
        : (control.options?.length ?? 0) > 0;
    return { ...control, type, show };
  })
  .filter((control) => control.show);
// The whole `.course-details__tier` block renders iff at least one control
// survived its own guard above — an authored `buybox` whose only control is
// e.g. an empty pill list correctly renders nothing, same as the old
// per-block guard did for a single control.
const showBuybox = buyboxControls.length > 0;
// Each select's accessible name defaults to the shared block `label` —
// BYTE-IDENTICAL to every already-shipped select-only buybox (Trainer
// Certification never authors `ariaLabel`, so this computes the exact same
// `aria-label` it always has). `control.ariaLabel` is an explicit, OPTIONAL
// override for the one case where that default is no longer safe: a select
// sharing its block label with an adjacent pill group (see the "A11Y"
// paragraph in this file's "Buy box controls generalization" header
// comment) — Functional Gait Assessment Level 1's own select is the only
// control that authors it today (`ariaLabel: 'Location'`, distinct from its
// block's shared "Course Type" label).
function selectAccessibleName(control, blockLabel) {
  return control.ariaLabel ?? blockLabel;
}
---
<section class="course-details" data-pdp-id={item.id}>
  <div class="course-details__inner">
    <p class="course-details__eyebrow"><span>Gait Happens</span></p>

    <div class="course-details__layout">
      <div class="course-details__buybox">
        <div class="course-details__heading">
          <h1 class="course-details__title">{item.pdp?.heroTitle ?? item.title}</h1>
          <div class="course-details__rating">
            <StarRating rating={item.rating ?? 0} count={item.reviewCount ?? 0} />
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="course-details__price">
          {item.pdp?.compareAtPrice ? (
            <>
              <span class="course-details__price-sale">{item.pdp?.priceExact ?? item.price}</span>
              <span class="course-details__price-compare">{item.pdp.compareAtPrice}</span>
            </>
          ) : (
            item.pdp?.priceExact ?? item.price
          )}
        </p>

        {item.pdp?.heroCaption ? (
          <div class="course-details__intro-block">
            <p class="course-details__caption">{item.pdp.heroCaption}</p>
            {item.pdp?.description && <p class="course-details__intro">{item.pdp.description}</p>}
          </div>
        ) : (
          item.pdp?.description && <p class="course-details__intro">{item.pdp.description}</p>
        )}

        {showBuybox && (
          <div class="course-details__tier">
            {buybox.label && <span class="course-details__tier-label">{buybox.label}</span>}
            {buyboxControls.map((control) =>
              control.type === 'select' ? (
                <select
                  class="course-details__select"
                  aria-label={selectAccessibleName(control, buybox.label)}
                >
                  <option value="" selected disabled hidden>{control.placeholder}</option>
                  {(control.options ?? []).map((option) => (
                    <option value={option.href ?? option.label}>{option.label}</option>
                  ))}
                </select>
              ) : (
                <div class="course-details__pills">
                  {control.options.map((option) =>
                    option.href ? (
                      <a
                        class={`course-details__pill${option.selected ? ' course-details__pill--selected' : ''}`}
                        href={option.href}
                        aria-current={option.selected ? 'true' : undefined}
                      >
                        {option.label}
                      </a>
                    ) : (
                      <span
                        class={`course-details__pill course-details__pill--static${option.selected ? ' course-details__pill--selected' : ''}`}
                      >
                        {option.label}
                        {option.selected && <span class="course-details__sr-only"> (selected)</span>}
                      </span>
                    )
                  )}
                </div>
              )
            )}
          </div>
        )}

        <a
          class="course-details__enroll"
          href={enrollHref}
          {...(isExternal ? { target: '_blank', rel: 'noopener noreferrer' } : {})}
        >
          {item.pdp?.enrollLabel ?? 'Enroll Now'}
        </a>

        {item.pdp?.instructorsByline && (
          <div class="course-details__instructors">
            <span class="course-details__avatars" aria-hidden="true">
              {(item.pdp.instructors ?? []).map(() => (
                <span class="course-details__avatar"></span>
              ))}
            </span>
            <p class="course-details__instructors-text">{item.pdp.instructorsByline}</p>
          </div>
        )}
      </div>

      {item.pdp?.courseCard ? (
        <div class={`course-details__card${item.pdp.courseCard.variant === 'yellow' ? ' course-details__card--yellow' : ''}`}>
          <p class="course-details__card-title">
            {item.pdp.courseCard.titleLines.map((line) => <span>{line}</span>)}
          </p>
          {item.pdp.courseCard.tag && <span class="course-details__card-tag">{item.pdp.courseCard.tag}</span>}
        </div>
      ) : item.pdp?.heroImage ? (
        <div class="course-details__hero-image">
          <img src={item.pdp.heroImage} alt="" />
        </div>
      ) : null}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<style>
  .course-details__inner {
    max-width: var(--container-max);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-block: var(--space-7);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }

  /* Same eyebrow markup/styling as ProductDetails.astro — Figma shows a
     plain caption ("GAIT HAPPENS"), not a navigational breadcrumb trail. */
  .course-details__eyebrow {
    font: var(--type-caption);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-caption);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
  .course-details__eyebrow span { display: inline-block; }

  .course-details__layout {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-6);
  }

  /* ---- Buy box ---------------------------------------------------- */

  .course-details__buybox {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    /* Figma's own buybox column gap (675:4353) is a uniform --space-5 (24px)
       between every child, unlike ProductDetails' --space-6 — kept
       frame-specific per the pulled design context. */
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }

  .course-details__heading {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }
  .course-details__title {
    font: var(--type-h3);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
  .course-details__rating {
    display: flex;
  }

  .course-details__price {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    font: var(--type-h4);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
  /* Optional sale/compare-at price — same fields/idiom as
     ProductDetails.astro's `.pdp-details__price-sale`/`-compare` (Figma
     `--feedback-error` #c62828, not yet a DS token; `--color-ink-70` for the
     struck-through original). Inert (no red, no strikethrough) whenever
     `item.pdp.compareAtPrice` is unset, e.g. Sole Switch Pro. */
  .course-details__price-sale {
    color: #c62828;
  }
  .course-details__price-compare {
    font: var(--type-body-large);
    color: var(--color-ink-70);
    text-decoration: line-through;
  }

  .course-details__intro {
    font: var(--type-body-sm);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* Optional hero caption ("*900+ Professionals Certified", Gait
     Foundations' 675:9037/1116:14701 frames — see header comment). Rendered
     only inside `.course-details__intro-block`, which exists only when
     `item.pdp.heroCaption` is set — absent everywhere else, so this rule is
     inert (matches no elements) on every other shipped PDP. Same font/colour
     as `.course-details__intro` (Figma uses one type style for both
     paragraphs); `.course-details__intro-block`'s own --space-2 (8px) column
     gap supplies the tight spacing Figma's `mb-[8px]` shows between them,
     independent of the buybox's outer --space-5 rhythm. */
  .course-details__intro-block {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-2);
  }
  .course-details__caption {
    font: var(--type-body-sm);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* Two-level structure mirroring Figma (675:8156;181:819 column > 181:822
     pill row) — see header comment ("Markup shape"). `-tier` is the column:
     label, then the pill row, --space-3 (12px) apart. `-pills` is its own
     flex-wrap row so the 8px pill-to-pill gap also governs the gap between
     WRAPPED pill rows (Fit Feet's 4-pill mobile case), matching Figma
     exactly instead of the 12px a flattened single-level container would
     apply there. */
  .course-details__tier {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }
  .course-details__tier-label {
    font: var(--type-caption);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-caption);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
  .course-details__pills {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-2);
  }

  /* ---- Select control (review fix wave: `pills.control === 'select'`) ----
     Trainer Certification's own "Course Type" node (683:10088;181:1352 /
     1116:17399;181:1388) is a bordered field with a "Location…" placeholder
     + chevron, not a pill row (see this file's header comment). A REAL
     native <select> — same "real, focusable, labeled control" precedent
     FilterSortBar.astro's own PLP filter/sort selects already establish for
     this repo, reusing that file's exact border/background/padding idiom
     rather than inventing a second styling approach — not a fake bordered
     `<div>`. No custom chevron overlay: FilterSortBar's own selects rely on
     the browser's native affordance with zero extra icon markup, and this
     follows that same precedent rather than adding `appearance: none` +
     a masked icon with no other precedent in this codebase. `239px` is a
     literal, measured off BOTH breakpoints' own "Input / Select" node
     (683:10088;181:1352;181:1320 desktop / 1116:17399;181:1388;181:1320
     mobile — identical on both, unlike most of this file's other
     desktop/mobile splits). */
  .course-details__select {
    width: 239px;
    max-width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    background: var(--color-paper);
    /* Figma's own placeholder text colour (`--text-muted,#837e7f`) is an
       exact hex match to this repo's `--color-border` token — reused here
       for the same value despite the "border" name, same "token regardless
       of its own name" allowance PdpAccordion.astro's `--type-nav-label`
       reuse already takes. */
    color: var(--color-border);
    font: var(--type-body-16);
    padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
  }

  /* Pill (secondary-CTA link OR inert language option) — outline style,
     matches ProductDetails' unselected size pill exactly (border-teal /
     paper bg / teal text). Shared by both real <a> links and the
     non-interactive <span>s (see header comment). */
  .course-details__pill {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: fit-content;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-teal);
    background: var(--color-paper);
    color: var(--color-teal);
    font: var(--type-cta);
    padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-5);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: transform var(--motion-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }
  /* Filled/selected state (Fit Feet's "English", 675:8156) — border stays
     the same 1px teal (now indistinguishable from the fill) so the box's
     rendered size doesn't shift versus its outline siblings on the same
     row. */
  .course-details__pill--selected {
    background: var(--color-teal);
    color: var(--color-paper);
  }
  /* Inert variant (no destination — see header comment). No pointer
     cursor; the shared hover-lift below is also scoped off real <a>
     elements only, so this gets no hover treatment either — same
     "decorative, not styled-inert-control" precedent Testimonial.astro's
     arrows already set. */
  .course-details__pill--static {
    cursor: default;
  }

  /* Visually-hidden text conveying the selected pill's state to screen
     readers — byte-for-byte the same idiom as `.comparison-chart__sr-only`
     / `.star-rating__sr-only` / `.testimonial__sr-only` (this repo's
     established visually-hidden utility, cited three times already). Used
     only inside the inert `<span>` branch (see header comment: `aria-current`
     on a role-less `<span>` isn't reliably surfaced by screen readers, and
     the fill-only distinction is otherwise colour-only/WCAG 1.4.1). The
     real `<a>` branch instead carries `aria-current="true"` directly —
     that's the canonically correct, universally-supported element for it. */
  .course-details__sr-only {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    padding: 0;
    margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }

  /* Primary CTA — Enroll (external, Kajabi). Same teal-filled styling as
     ProductDetails' Add to Cart button (this frame's colors match — teal,
     not yellow, confirmed via the pulled screenshot, not assumed). */
  .course-details__enroll {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 100%;
    background: var(--color-teal);
    color: var(--color-paper);
    font: var(--type-button);
    padding: var(--space-4) 20px;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: transform var(--motion-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  /* Hover-lift only applies to real links — tag-qualified so the inert
     language `<span>`s (see header comment) don't inherit an affordance
     they don't have; CSS :hover fires on any hovered element regardless of
     interactivity, so this can't rely on the class alone. */
  a.course-details__pill:hover,
  .course-details__enroll:hover {
    transform: translateY(-2px);
  }

  .course-details__instructors {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }
  /* The avatar pair stays a side-by-side row at both widths (6px apart per
     Figma); only the OUTER container flips to column on desktop so the pair
     sits above the byline. */
  .course-details__avatars {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 6px;
  }
  /* Flat placeholder circles — no real instructor photography yet; Task 7
     ("Your Instructors") owns real bios/photos further down the page. Fill
     reuses --color-border, the same "no photography yet" placeholder token
     ProductDetails' gallery media background already uses. */
  .course-details__avatar {
    width: 42px;
    height: 42px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--color-border);
    flex: 0 0 auto;
  }
  .course-details__instructors-text {
    font: var(--type-body-sm);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* ---- Branded course card (right column) ---------------------------- */

  .course-details__card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    /* --space-5 (not --space-7) on mobile: the card is a literal fixed
       210px-tall block there (Figma 1109:14377;181:674), and the 2-line
       --type-display title + tag pill need ~137px — --space-7 (48px, 96px
       total) would leave only 114px and risk overflowing that fixed height.
       Desktop has far more room (see aspect-ratio below) so it switches to
       the frame's literal --space-7 padding. */
    padding: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-teal);
    height: 210px;
  }
  /* Optional colour variant — unlike NavCard.astro's `nav-card--${variant}`
     interpolation (which accepts any variant string), this component only
     ever toggles a single fixed 'yellow' modifier class (see the header
     comment). Sole Switch's card (998:14856) is yellow with
     no tag; Sole Switch Pro's stays the base teal (no `variant` set) +
     its "For Health Professionals" tag. Figma's own flattened card raster
     confirms the title text stays white on both colours, so only the
     background changes here. */
  .course-details__card--yellow {
    background: var(--color-yellow);
  }
  .course-details__card-title {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    font: var(--type-display);
    color: var(--color-paper);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    line-height: 1.05;
  }
  .course-details__card-tag {
    display: inline-block;
    width: fit-content;
    background: var(--color-yellow);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font: var(--type-label);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-caption);
    padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
  }

  /* ---- Hero image (right column) — mutually exclusive with the branded
     card above (see header comment). Mobile is a literal Figma-measured
     fixed 210px height, identical to `.course-details__card`'s own mobile
     height (999:7156;181:674 is the same slot/frame as the card's). */
  .course-details__hero-image {
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--color-border);
    height: 210px;
  }
  .course-details__hero-image img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    a.course-details__pill:hover,
    .course-details__enroll:hover {
      transform: none;
    }
  }

  /* ---- Desktop (Figma 675:4353, ≥1024) ------------------------------- */
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .course-details__layout {
      flex-direction: row;
      align-items: flex-start;
      gap: var(--space-8);
    }
    .course-details__buybox {
      flex: 0 0 484px;
      max-width: 484px;
    }
    /* Desktop (675:4353) stacks the avatar pair ABOVE the "Course By:"
       byline text; the base row layout is the mobile (1109:14377)
       arrangement. */
    .course-details__instructors {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: flex-start;
    }
    /* Only the enroll CTA needs the override — the outline pill is already
       width: fit-content in its base rule. */
    .course-details__enroll {
      width: fit-content;
    }
    .course-details__card {
      flex: 1 0 0;
      align-self: stretch;
      /* Card geometry measured off the 675:4353 frame itself: 1200 canvas −
         2×48 padding − 484 buybox − 64 gap = 556 wide; content-row height
         ≈399 (531 frame − 2×48 padding − eyebrow row). Fixed aspect-ratio
         (not the mobile fixed height) keeps the card proportional as the
         real container grows past Figma's 1200 canvas up to --container-max
         (1440) — same flexible-width/fixed-ratio approach ProductDetails'
         gallery media uses. */
      height: auto;
      aspect-ratio: 556 / 399;
      /* Desktop has ample height (≥399px) for the frame's own --space-7
         (48px) padding — mobile's smaller --space-5 above is a fixed-height
         fit constraint, not a desktop concern. */
      padding: var(--space-7);
    }
    /* Desktop hero image (675:5542;173:147) is unconstrained in Figma — no
       fixed height/aspect-ratio, just `self-stretch` — so unlike the card
       above this intentionally carries no aspect-ratio literal, letting it
       stretch to match the buybox's actual rendered height directly (see
       header comment). */
    .course-details__hero-image {
      flex: 1 0 0;
      align-self: stretch;
      height: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
